{"meta":{"id":"adria_series_ia_the-world-at-war-1973-thames-television-world-war-two","type":"series","name":"The World at War (1973) - Thames Television","poster":"https://archive.org/services/img/the-world-at-war-1973-thames-television-world-war-two","posterShape":"landscape","background":"https://archive.org/services/img/the-world-at-war-1973-thames-television-world-war-two","description":"📦 Origem: Internet Archive (coleção aberta — confira a licença)\n\nThe\nWorld At War (1973)\n\nProducer -\nThames Television - Jeremy Isaacs\n\nDirector -\nDavid Elstein\n\nNarrator\n- Laurence Olivier\n\nComposer -\nCarl Davis\n\n1 Series -\n26 Episodes\n\nRun Time -\n22 hours 32 minutes\n\nOriginal\nbroadcast network - ITV\n\nOriginal\nRelease 31 October 1973 - 8 May 1974\n\nThe World\nat War is a 26-episode British documentary television series that\nchronicles the events of the Second World War. It was produced in\n1973, at a cost of £900,000 (equivalent to £11,600,000 in 2021),\nthe most expensive factual series ever produced. It was produced by\nJeremy Isaacs, narrated by Laurence Olivier and included music\ncomposed by Carl Davis. The book, The World at War, published the\nsame year, was written by Mark Arnold-Forster to accompany the TV\nseries.\n\nThe World\nat War attracted widespread acclaim and now it is regarded as a\nlandmark in British television history. The series focused on a\nportrayal of the experience of the conflict: of how life and death\nthroughout the war years affected soldiers, sailors and airmen,\ncivilians, concentration camp inmates and other victims of the war.\n\n01 A New\nGermany ( 1933– 1939) [31 October 1973]\n\n02 Distant\nWar (September 1939 – May 1940) [7…","releaseInfo":"1973","year":"1973","genres":["The world at war","Second world war","Documentary","Television series"],"links":[{"name":"Fonte original","category":"Origem","url":"https://archive.org/details/the-world-at-war-1973-thames-television-world-war-two"}],"videos":[{"id":"adria_s